   104	## 3. Follow-up durability scope (separate issue, re-scoped per reviewers)
   105	
   106	This is the ONLY work that should ship, and only as defence-in-depth — NOT
   107	as a forwarding fix. All three reviewer corrections are folded:
   108	
   109	1. **`queue_count` = `max(QueueID where Bound && XSKRegistered) + 1`** —
   110	   NOT count-of-ready, NOT keyed on `Ready`.
   111	   - Reviewer basis: a scalar `queue_count` only steers correctly when the
   112	     deliverable set is a contiguous prefix `0..N-1`. Using the *count*
   113	     (e.g. 3 for bound {0,1,3}) maps a packet on queue 3 to `3%3=0` whose
   114	     XSK is on queue 0 → kernel drops (`xs->queue_id != queue_id`) — AGY r1
   115	     §2.1, Codex r1 #6. Using `max(BOUND)+1` keeps the prefix semantics and
   116	     stops a *registered-but-unbound* tail queue from inflating the modulo.
   117	   - Keying on `Bound && XSKRegistered` (not `Ready`) avoids `Ready`'s
   118	     `heartbeat_fresh` leg making `queue_count` flap with worker liveness
   119	     (Codex r1 #7, AGY r1 §2.1). It is independent of the `enabled` gate
   120	     (which keys on `armed`), so it does not reintroduce the
   121	     ctrl=0→no-RX→not-ready bootstrap deadlock (`helpers.rs:207-209`).
   122	   - **Hard limitation (must be documented, not worked around):** an
   123	     *interior* hole (bound {0,2,3}, queue 1 unbound) cannot be represented
   124	     by any scalar `queue_count`. `max(BOUND)+1 = 4` re-strands queue 1;
   125	     a smaller value mis-steers queues 2/3. A correct interior-hole fix
   126	     needs a per-queue deliverable **bitmap** in the ctrl/binding map (shim
   127	     consults `ready[q]` before steering), or channel pinning so holes
   128	     can't form. Bitmap is the clean design if interior holes ever occur in
   129	     practice; on virtio they don't (contiguous bind). Scope the bitmap
   130	     OUT of the first follow-up; document the prefix assumption.
   131	
   132	2. **Stranded-queue transit policy: fail-CLOSED (XDP_DROP) by default,
   133	   health-visible.** REJECT r1's II-c PASS-to-kernel default.
   134	   - Reviewer basis: `architecture.md:27-29` mandates degraded non-local
   135	     transit "fails closed in both compat and strict modes instead of
   136	     bypassing policy, NAT, or conntrack"; `ModeUserspaceCompat` is
   137	     documented "degraded transit fails closed" (`manager.go:41`). PASS is
   138	     a silent security + correctness regression (no SNAT → asymmetric, TCP
   139	     hangs) — Codex r1 #8, AGY r1 §2.3, SMR r1 F2.
   140	   - Keep the existing DROP; the durability win is **observability**: a
   141	     distinct fallback reason + counter + Prometheus gauge
   142	     (`xpf_userspace_stranded_queue_drops`) + a `show` surface so a future
   143	     partial bind is loud, not silent. (PASS could be a future explicit
   144	     operator opt-in with a loud health-red, but is NOT the default and NOT
   145	     in this scope.)
   146	
   147	3. **Adaptive queue-drop: do NOT extend the global rebind watchdog.**
   148	   - Reviewer basis: `hasBusyBindingsWedgeLocked` (`maps_sync.go:1285`)
   149	     only fires when `bound==0` — a partial bind (3/4) never triggers it,
