   192	The remainder of this section and §6's `effective_rx_queues` are **Phase-2
   193	design notes (contingent, design-open)** — do not implement without a fresh
   194	review round confirming Phase 1 was insufficient.
   195	
   196	### [PHASE-2 DESIGN NOTE, CONTINGENT] Path A — reconcile NIC channels to the uniform `target`
   197	
   198	(`target = min(workers, hw-max)` per the §6 uniform invariant; the lines below
   199	say `workers` for the common appliance case but the binding/RSS count is always
   200	the reconciled `target`.) Pin `ethtool -L <dataplane-if> combined = target`
   201	(clamped to the NIC's
   202	hardware max) **before** any XSK bind, at clean helper startup and after
   203	`stop_workers` on reconcile. Then bind exactly queues `0..workers-1`
   204	(`queueCountFromBindings` then reports `workers`, keeping the shim steering
   205	consistent). Effects:
   206	- NIC delivers only on `workers` RSS queues, all of which are owned by a socket
   207	  that binds → no flow is RSS-hashed to a queue whose binding never goes READY
   208	  (which the shim would drop as `binding_not_ready`, `lib.rs:427`).
   209	- Fewer queues = smaller EBUSY surface and fewer sockets to leak across a
   210	  rebind. (This does NOT by itself prevent the EBUSY race on the surviving
   211	  queues — see teardown co-primary fix and the §3 caveat. It is not a
   212	  whole-dataplane "gate" effect: per the r1 correction, `armed` is a request
   213	  flag, so a failed bind does not trip `enabled`/`probeBindingsReady`.)
   214	- Ordering avoids the "channels too low for existing ZC sockets" refusal: set
   215	  channels only when no XSK is bound (fresh start, or post-`stop_workers`).
   216	- mlx5/i40e: `workers` already == queue count, so `combined=workers` is a no-op
   217	  → no regression.
   218	- **Caveat:** this reduces the bound set but does not by itself prove the EBUSY
   219	  is gone — if the EBUSY is a stale-socket race (§3 hypothesis a), it can still
   220	  fire on the `0..workers-1` set across a reconcile. Phase 0 must confirm.
   221	
   222	Tradeoff: virtio default `workers=1` caps the dataplane to one queue (≈ one
   223	core of AF_XDP throughput). Acceptable for the "labs/modest WAN" positioning;
   224	operators raise `set system … workers N` for more. Implementation touches:
   225	`replan_queues` (its queue count comes from the reconciled `effective_rx_queues`
   226	in the snapshot — see §6 for the uniform-target constraint), a new
   227	channel-reconciliation step in the Go control plane applied to **every**
   228	dataplane NIC (not just virtio — see hazard 1) ordered before snapshot build,
   229	and docs.
   230	
   231	### Path B — default `workers` to the NIC RX-queue count
   232	
   233	When `workers` is unset, derive it from `rx_queue_count` so `queue_count ==
   234	queues`, bind all, RSS uses all. Full parallelism, no channel mutation.
   235	Tradeoffs: higher default CPU (4 workers on a 4-vCPU box leaves nothing for the
   236	control plane); does NOT address Bug 2's EBUSY if it is a stale-socket race
   237	(orthogonal); a NIC with more queues than vCPUs over-subscribes. Weaker venue
   238	fit for the "modest" default.
   239	
   240	### Path C — degraded-mode enable + RSS-to-bound-subset
   241	
   242	Keep over-provisioning but (a) change the enable gate to "≥1 armed binding per
   243	forwarding interface" and (b) constrain RSS indirection (`ethtool -X`) to the
   244	successfully-bound queues, and (c) harden the shim fallback to redirect by
   245	actual `rx_queue_index` whenever the selected binding's bound ring mismatches.
   246	Most general (tolerates partial binds on any driver) but the largest semantic
   247	change: "enabled" could mask real partial-bind failures, and RSS reprogramming
   248	mid-life is fiddly. Higher risk; recommend only as defense-in-depth on top of A.
   249	
   250	### Recommendation (supersedes the co-primary framing above)
   251	
   252	Commit only **Phase 1 (the rebind double-stop fix)** plus **Phase 0** to prove
   253	it. It is small, code-verified, and independently corrects two latent rebind
   254	bugs. Phase 2 (channel/RSS reconciliation) is contingent and design-open per the
   255	§5 intro — do NOT bundle it into the committed PR unless Phase 0/1 prove the
   256	binds still fail after the rebind fix. The decision rule:
   257	
   258	- Phase 1 makes the `0..actual_rx_count` set bind cleanly (the expected outcome
   259	  once the quiesce is no longer bypassed) → **DONE**; no reconciliation, no
   260	  ring-mismatch (queue_count == actual), no surplus.
   261	- Phase 1 still leaves queues unbindable for a non-stale-socket reason → open a
   262	  Phase-2 design round for queue reconciliation, resolving the
   263	  uniform-vs-per-interface planner + fabric-parent question first.
   264	
   265	Phase B (default workers = RX-queue count) and Phase C (degraded enable) are
   266	documented as alternatives but NOT recommended.
   267	
   268	### [PHASE-2 DESIGN NOTE, CONTINGENT] Implementation hazards if reconciliation is ever needed
   269	
   270	1. **Driver-agnostic, operator-gated reconciliation, never a silent reduction**
   271	   (Codex #4, SMR F1). If we plan `0..workers` but pin channels only on virtio,
   272	   any NIC with `workers < hw RX queues` (e.g. an operator setting `workers=2`
   273	   on a 6-queue mlx5 VF) gets the ring-mismatch back: RSS spreads to queues with
   274	   no socket, `queue_count=workers` makes the modulo map them onto the wrong
   275	   ring → kernel drop. The reconciliation (channel/RSS = bound set) must apply
   276	   to **every** dataplane NIC, with explicit "`workers` = desired queue fanout"
   277	   semantics — and must never reduce a NIC below the fanout the operator
   278	   intends. `rss_indirection.go:164` already special-cases mlx5 workers==1;
   279	   integrate, don't fight it.
   280	2. **`ethtool -L` ordering** (Codex #3). Rust trusts `snapshot.rx_queues`
   281	   (`helpers.rs:698`), stamped by Go from sysfs (`interfaces.go:173`). The
   282	   channel set MUST happen in Go **before** snapshot construction, or the
   283	   snapshot must carry the explicitly-reconciled count. A helper-side pin after
   284	   snapshot receipt is too late.
   285	3. **`ethtool -L` async-teardown retry** (AGY #3). Running `-L` right after a
   286	   helper stop can fail while the kernel is still asynchronously tearing down ZC
   287	   socket contexts — wait/retry with backoff.
   288	4. **Non-fatal channel set, but keep the target uniform** (AGY #4). Many VF
   289	   drivers don't support `ethtool -L`; an `-L` failure must NOT fail-close the
   290	   dataplane. But the fallback is NOT "proceed with this NIC's actual count"
   291	   (that breaks the uniform planner and re-creates wrong-ring): fall back to
   292	   `ethtool -X` RSS-constrain to `0..target-1`; if neither `-L` nor `-X` works
   293	   and the NIC natively delivers beyond `target`, that is the documented
   294	   homogeneous-constrainable-NIC limit (§6). The snapshot is stamped with the
   295	   uniform verified `target` (transactional, per §6) regardless.
   296	5. **Fabric/IPVLAN parents: `-X` not `-L`, and must ALSO be constrained**
   297	   (resolves Codex r4 blocker + AGY r4 clarification). Fabric parents ARE
   298	   AF_XDP-bound ingress interfaces — Rust adds them as binding candidates
   299	   (`helpers.rs:709-726`) and Go marks them userspace-ingress
   300	   (`maps_sync.go:1430`). So excluding them from reconciliation entirely would
   301	   leave them RSS-delivering on `> target` queues → the wrong-ring drop on the
   302	   fabric NIC. Resolution: exclude fabric parents from `ethtool -L` only (it
   303	   resets the link → VRRP/control + IPVLAN-child disruption), but STILL
   304	   constrain their RSS via `ethtool -X` to `0..target-1` (no link reset). Go
   305	   stamps the fabric parent's snapshot `rx_queues = target` so the global-min
   306	   planner (`helpers.rs:745`) computes the intended uniform `target` rather than
   307	   being pulled up/down by an unreconciled fabric count (AGY r4). Phase 0 must
   308	   measure the fabric parent's actual RX-queue count and whether its (generic
   309	   XDP, HA-only, low-volume) ingress is RSS-distributed at all — if it is
   310	   single-queue, the constraint is trivially satisfied; validate fabric TX still
   311	   works after `-X`.
   312	7. **Drivers reset channels/RSS to defaults on link-UP** (AGY r4, new). After a
   313	   RETH-MAC link cycle (link DOWN→UP), mlx5/virtio_net revert channel counts and
   314	   the RSS indirection table to hardware defaults. If Go issues `rebind` without
   315	   first re-applying the channel/RSS pinning, the helper binds `0..target-1` but
   316	   the NIC again spreads across all queues → wrong-ring/EBUSY. Go MUST re-run
   317	   the channel/RSS reconciliation inside `NotifyLinkCycle`
   318	   (`process.go:1018-1065`) after the 1s settle and BEFORE issuing the `rebind`
   319	   ControlRequest.
   320	6. **Auto-rebind must do teardown-before-rebind, or it loops** (refines AGY #2;
   321	   AGY's "blind spot" framing was REFUTED by Codex r2 and verified false). The
   322	   busy-bindings watchdog DOES fire on the all-queues-EBUSY case:
   323	   `hasBusyBindingsWedgeLocked` (`maps_sync.go:1265-1285`) triggers on
   324	   `registeredArmed > 0 && bound == 0 && ready == 0 && busyErr`, and
   325	   `registeredArmed` counts any registered+armed binding — which a failed-bind
   326	   queue still is (`armed` is a request flag), so the wedge is detected (unit
   327	   test `manager_test.go:2943` covers queue-0-busy). The real concern is that
   328	   the auto-rebind path re-hits the same stale-socket EBUSY and re-wedges; the
   329	   teardown-before-rebind fix must therefore live in the rebind path too, not
   330	   just first bind.
   331	
   332	## 6. API / interface preservation
   333	
   334	- No control-protocol wire change required for Path A: `ctrl.queue_count` and
   335	  `workers` already exist; we change how the bound set + NIC channels are
   336	  derived, not the schema. (Confirm whether a new snapshot field is needed to
   337	  carry "desired channels" vs computing in the helper.)
   338	- **[PHASE-2 DESIGN NOTE, CONTINGENT — not part of the committed Phase-1 fix;
   339	  design-open per §5, needs its own review round.] `effective_rx_queues`
   340	  invariant — UNIFORM across dataplane NICs.** Known-unresolved: this collides
   341	  with the global-min planner enumerating fabric parents (a single-queue fabric
   342	  parent stamped to `target>1` would bind nonexistent queues — Codex r5). A
   343	  per-interface bind-count planner or fabric split-out must be decided first.
   344	  Critical constraint:
   345	  `replan_bindings_from_candidates` (`helpers.rs:745`) takes the GLOBAL MINIMUM
   346	  queue count across all candidate interfaces and emits `0..queue_count` for
