commit 016bc763482276886572ce5a2192ae4ba4fc3186
Author: Paul Saab <ps@mu.org>
Date:   Mon Jun 15 18:16:44 2026 -0700

    #1921: dedup replan candidates by Linux netdev (the virtio-MQ forwarding fix)
    
    Live capstone on a 4-queue virtio appliance revealed the rebind double-stop fix
    was necessary but NOT sufficient: forwarding stayed at 0 because the binding
    plan contained 16 entries for 8 real (ifindex, queue_id) pairs — EVERY queue
    double-bound. The /metrics binding map confirmed the pattern exactly:
    slots 0,1 = ge-0-0-0 q0; 2,3 = ge-0-0-1 q0; 4,5 = ge-0-0-0 q1; ... i.e.
    candidates = [ge-0-0-0, ge-0-0-0, ge-0-0-1, ge-0-0-1].
    
    Root cause: the control-plane snapshot emits BOTH the physical interface
    (`ge-0/0/0`) and its unit (`ge-0/0/0.0`) as separate InterfaceSnapshot entries
    (pkg/dataplane/userspace/interfaces.go). Both names start with `ge-`, and for a
    non-VLAN unit both resolve to the SAME Linux netdev (`ge-0-0-0`).
    replan_queues pushed each as a candidate WITHOUT deduping by linux_name — the
    seen_linux set was only consulted in the fabric-parent loop, not the interface
    loop. The duplicate produced a second binding for every (ifindex, queue_id); the
    second XSK bind on an already-bound queue returns EBUSY, the queue never goes
    READY, and the shim drops all transit on it -> 0 forwarding.
    
    Fix: skip an interface candidate whose linux_name was already seen, exactly as
    the fabric loop already does. One XSK per (netdev, queue) is the AF_XDP
    contract.
    
    Adds queue_planner_dedups_physical_and_unit_to_same_netdev (verified it fails
    without the guard: 8 bindings instead of 4, with duplicate (ifindex,queue_id)
    pairs). This pairs with the rebind double-stop fix; together they close the
    virtio multi-queue forwarding outage.
